  <p class="lede">The bridge solved the question of whether a Python policy could see the game at all. The next question was what, exactly, it should see — and in what shape.</p>

  <p>The C# side ships a JSON dict with maybe forty keys at the top level and arbitrarily long lists inside it. The policy is a fixed-shape MLP that wants a <code>[B, 583]</code> float tensor and a <code>[B, 61]</code> boolean action mask. Everything between those two ends is encoding policy.</p>

  <p>This is the article about that encoding. It lives in <code>src/bot/model.py</code> — <code>EncodedState</code>, <code>encode_state()</code>, and the small set of constants at the top of the file that pin down every dimension the network ever sees. The decisions are dense: which fields get their own scalar slot, which get folded into a learned embedding, which get mean-pooled into a bag of features, which get padded to a fixed length and which get clamped at a tail. Most of those decisions started somewhere else and got dragged into their current shape one blindspot at a time.</p>

  <h2 id="contract"><span class="num">§ 1</span>The contract — 583 floats and 61 booleans</h2>
  <svg class="under" viewBox="0 0 90 8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M 2 4 Q 18 1, 32 5 T 60 3 T 86 5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>

  <div class="exhibit-index">
    <div class="ei-bar">⟢ exhibit index ⟣</div>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="ei-num">EXHIBIT 1</span><span class="ei-dim">18 dims</span><span class="ei-name">the scalar block</span></li>
      <li><span class="ei-num">EXHIBIT 2</span><span class="ei-dim">270 dims</span><span class="ei-name">the hand block</span></li>
      <li><span class="ei-num">EXHIBIT 3</span><span class="ei-dim">252 dims</span><span class="ei-name">the enemy block</span></li>
      <li><span class="ei-num">EXHIBIT 4</span><span class="ei-dim">24 dims</span><span class="ei-name">the pile pools</span></li>
      <li><span class="ei-num">EXHIBIT 5</span><span class="ei-dim">19 dims</span><span class="ei-name">the trailing pools (relics + powers)</span></li>
      <li class="ei-divider">— sums to 583 trunk-input dims —</li>
      <li><span class="ei-num">EXHIBIT 6</span><span class="ei-dim">61 bits</span><span class="ei-name">the action mask · output side</span></li>
      <li><span class="ei-num">EXHIBIT 7</span><span class="ei-dim">—</span><span class="ei-name">the full map · closer</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>The shape is fixed at the constants block of <code>model.py</code>:</p>

  <div class="codeblock">
    <div class="code-file">src/bot/model.py · constants</div>
<pre><code>{`MAX_HAND      = 10
MAX_TARGETS   = 6
MAX_RELICS    = 20
MAX_DRAW      = 30
MAX_DISCARD   = 30
MAX_EXHAUST   = 30
MAX_POWERS    = 15
MAX_ACTIONS   = 1 + MAX_HAND * MAX_TARGETS  # 61

CARD_EMB_DIM  = 16
ENEMY_EMB_DIM = 16
RELIC_EMB_DIM = 8
PILE_EMB_DIM  = 8
POWER_EMB_DIM = 8
HIDDEN_DIM    = 256`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>Worked end-to-end, the trunk consumes:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>18</strong> scalar floats for the player and turn</li>
    <li><strong>270</strong> floats for the hand (10 cards × (16-dim card embedding + 11 extra features))</li>
    <li><strong>252</strong> floats for enemies (6 enemies × (16-dim enemy embedding + 17 scalars + 9-dim mean-pooled per-enemy power slot))</li>
    <li><strong>24</strong> floats for the three piles, each compressed to an 8-dim mean-pooled bag</li>
    <li><strong>10</strong> floats for the mean-pooled relic pool (8-dim embedding + 2 scalars)</li>
    <li><strong>9</strong> floats for the mean-pooled player generic-power pool</li>
  </ul>

  <p>That is 583 features into the trunk, two layers of <code>Linear → LayerNorm → ReLU</code> at width 256, then three heads: a 61-way policy logit head, a scalar value head, and a 10-way discard-priority head. Every choice in the encoder is constrained by that shape contract. If hands had a hard maximum of 12 instead of 10, the policy head would be 73-way instead of 61-way. If enemies had 7 slots instead of 6, the trunk input would be 625 instead of 583. None of these are inferable from a JSON dict; they are bets, and changing one of them is a network rebuild.</p>

  <p><code>MAX_HAND=10</code> is a generous bound on a typical Silent hand. <code>MAX_TARGETS=6</code> is one slot more than any combat the curriculum currently runs. <code>MAX_RELICS=20</code> is well above any realistic mid-act inventory. <code>MAX_DRAW=30</code> and friends are deliberately tight: the bot's training decks rarely hit 30 cards in a single pile, and the network would rather not budget for long-tail draws it never sees.</p>

  <p>The thing that surprised me, in retrospect, is how little of the 583 is the part that "feels like" the game state. The 18 scalars cover the bookkeeping a player tracks at a glance — HP, block, energy, pile sizes, debuff stacks. The other 565 floats are almost entirely cards: 10 cards in hand, 90 anonymous cards across three piles, plus a small allowance for relic and power identities. The policy spends an order of magnitude more of its input width on "what is in your deck right now" than on "how is your character doing." That ratio matches the game — Slay-style deckbuilders are about the deck, not the character — but it took building the encoder to feel that in the dimensions.</p>

  <h2 id="scalars"><span class="num">§ 2</span>The 18 scalars — where every "weird" decision lives</h2>

  <p>The scalar block is the most decisional sliver of the input. Eighteen floats, each a deliberate choice about what shape the network can recognize. Pulled straight from <code>encode_state</code>:</p>

  <div class="codeblock">
    <div class="code-file">src/bot/model.py · encode_state · scalars</div>
<pre><code>{`scalars = torch.tensor([[
    player["hp"]     / max(player["max_hp"], 1),
    player["block"]  / max(player["max_hp"], 1),
    player["energy"] / max(player["max_energy"], 1),
    state.get("draw_size", 0) / 30.0,
    state.get("discard_size", 0) / 30.0,
    state.get("exhaust_size", 0) / 30.0,
    min(turn, 20) / 20.0,
    len(hand) / MAX_HAND,
    _norm(player.get("strength", 0),   -300, 300),
    _norm(player.get("dexterity", 0),   -50,  50),
    _norm(player.get("vulnerable", 0),    0, 300),
    _norm(player.get("weak", 0),          0,  50),
    _norm(player.get("frail", 0),         0, 100),
    _norm(player.get("intangible", 0),    0,  20),
    _norm(player.get("thorns", 0),        0,  50),
    _norm(player.get("attacks_played", 0), 0, 10),
    _norm(player.get("skills_played", 0),  0, 10),
    _norm(player.get("powers_played", 0),  0,  5),
]], dtype=torch.float32)`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <figure class="fig">
    <div class="exhibit">
      <div class="exhibit-bar">
        <span class="file">model.py · scalars [B, 18]</span>
        <span class="num-pill">EXHIBIT 1</span>
      </div>
      <div class="exhibit-body">
        <div class="scalar-3x6">
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">hp_frac</div><div class="frm">/max_hp</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">block_frac</div><div class="frm">/max_hp</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">energy_frac</div><div class="frm">/max_e</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">draw_frac</div><div class="frm">/30</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">discard_frac</div><div class="frm">/30</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">exhaust_frac</div><div class="frm">/30</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell dec"><div class="lbl">turn_norm</div><div class="frm">min(t,20)/20</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">hand_frac</div><div class="frm">/MAX_HAND</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell dec"><div class="lbl">strength</div><div class="frm">_norm(±300)</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">dexterity</div><div class="frm">_norm(±50)</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">vulnerable</div><div class="frm">_norm(0,300)</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell dec"><div class="lbl">weak</div><div class="frm">_norm(0,50)</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">frail</div><div class="frm">_norm(0,100)</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">intangible</div><div class="frm">_norm(0,20)</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">thorns</div><div class="frm">_norm(0,50)</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">attacks_p</div><div class="frm">_norm(0,10)</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">skills_p</div><div class="frm">_norm(0,10)</div></div>
          <div class="scalar-cell"><div class="lbl">powers_p</div><div class="frm">_norm(0,5)</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="exhibit-side">
        <div class="placard-title">Decisional cells</div>
        <ul>
          <li><span class="bli-tag">BLI 8</span>turn_norm — saturated past turn 20.</li>
          <li>strength — asymmetric <code class="placard-code">_norm</code>, anchored at 0.</li>
          <li>weak — dedicated, not pooled. 25% damage debuff is too load-bearing for a bag.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="fig-caption"><span class="figtag">Exhibit 1</span>Eighteen floats. Three decisions worth defending.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The first six are obvious — fractions in <code>[0, 1]</code>. The seventh is the one I want to flag: <code>min(turn, 20) / 20.0</code>. Turn number saturates at 20. This is a known blindspot, written up explicitly in <code>docs/blindspot.md</code> (#8). Any combat past turn 20 looks identical to the network. The reason it's still there is reward-side: the per-turn penalty in <code>reward.py</code> already discourages dragging combats out, and the curriculum's combats almost never run that long. The cost of fixing it (longer encoding, more features near the tail of a long-tail distribution) wasn't worth the gain. Saturation is the right answer when the saturation point is past the support of the data.</p>

  <p>The interesting decisions are below the fold, in the buff/debuff block. Five player buffs — Strength, Dexterity, Vulnerable, Weak, Frail — get their own dedicated scalar slot. Two combat counters (Intangible, Thorns) join them. Three turn-action counters (<code>attacks_played</code>, <code>skills_played</code>, <code>powers_played</code>) join those. Every other power on the player gets routed through the generic mean-pooled power slot at the end of the input — about which more in a moment.</p>

  <p>The "should this power be dedicated or generic?" question is the one I revisited the most. The answer ended up being a small set rule: if a power's optimal-play implication is fundamentally different from "it's a number that goes up or down," it gets a dedicated scalar. Strength changes how every attack is valued. Vulnerable changes how every attack on the enemy is valued. Weak attaches a 25% damage debuff to my own attacks. These need precise visibility; the network can't afford to learn them through a noisy mean-pooled bag — and Strength, Dexterity, Vulnerable, Weak, Frail, Intangible, and Thorns are the seven the Python encoder currently consumes as their own scalar slots.</p>

  <p>The C# <code>PlayerDedicated</code> set in <code>StateSerializer.cs</code> is actually wider than that. It also names <code>BarricadePower</code> and <code>BlurPower</code> — the block-persistence powers — because block-persistence fundamentally changes optimal play and the C# author (me, on a different day) didn't want them mean-pooled. But the Python encoder doesn't currently have scalar slots for <code>barricade</code> or <code>blur</code>. They get excluded from the generic pool by <code>PlayerDedicated</code>, so they are sent on the wire and then dropped on the floor at the encoder. This is the same pattern as the extra intent flags below, and the same pattern as <code>eternal</code> on cards: the C# schema runs ahead of the Python encoder, on the theory that adding fields C#-side is free and the encoder catches up when a curriculum stage actually needs them.</p>

  <p>The non-obvious decision is <code>_norm</code>. Most ML codebases use a single <code>clamp_then_scale_to_[0,1]</code> helper. This one doesn't:</p>

  <div class="codeblock">
    <div class="code-file">src/bot/model.py · _norm</div>
<pre><code>{`def _norm(val, lo, hi):
    """
    Clamp val to [lo, hi] and map to [lo/hi, 1.0].
    When lo=0 the output is [0, 1].
    When lo<0 (e.g. strength: lo=-300, hi=300) the output is [-1, 1] with
    zero mapping to 0.0 — NOT to the midpoint of the range. That asymmetry
    is intentional: positive and negative strength are each scaled by 1/hi.
    """
    return min(max(val, lo), hi) / hi`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>The asymmetry matters. Strength can go negative (Flex's downside, Choke effects, Decay — Silent has a few). The naive normalization — <code>(val - lo) / (hi - lo)</code> — would map <code>strength=0</code> to <code>0.5</code>, the midpoint of the <code>[lo, hi]</code> interval. The network would then have to learn that <code>0.5</code> is "neutral" and that <code>&lt; 0.5</code> is "I am weakened." That's solvable with enough samples, but it makes the gradient on a feature that should be massively load-bearing pass through a learned bias near <code>0.5</code>. With the asymmetric <code>_norm</code>, <code>strength=0</code> is a literal zero in the input vector. <code>strength=+5</code> is <code>+5/300 = 0.0166</code>. <code>strength=-5</code> is <code>-0.0166</code>. Zero is anchored. The network gets the sign of strength for free, the magnitude in the right scale, and doesn't have to spend any capacity on "where is neutral."</p>

  <p>It's a tiny thing — six characters of code, one division, no clamp — and it's the difference between a network that knows it has negative Strength and one that thinks zero-Strength is half-bad.</p>

  <h2 id="hand"><span class="num">§ 3</span>The hand — padded, embedded, and 11 extra features per card</h2>

  <p>The hand is the densest part of the input. Each of <code>MAX_HAND=10</code> slots gets:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>A 16-dim learned card embedding, looked up via <code>card_vocab.index("StrikeSilent")</code> or <code>card_vocab.index("StrikeSilent+")</code> for an upgraded copy.</li>
    <li>11 extra scalar features per card: cost (normalized by 3), upgraded flag, three card-type one-hots (attack/skill/power), an x-cost flag, and five keyword flags (exhaust, ethereal, innate, retain, sly).</li>
    <li>A <code>card_mask</code> slot that is <code>1.0</code> for a real card and <code>0.0</code> for a padding slot — used to zero out padding contributions before the trunk concat.</li>
  </ul>

