    <figcaption class="fig-caption"><span class="figtag">Exhibit 2</span>One slot in detail, then ten of them stacked. The 11 extras carry the side-channel of fixed mechanics — Strike+ deals 9, but the fact that Strike is a 1-cost attack is hardcoded.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The 11 extras are a direct response to blindspot #4 in <code>docs/blindspot.md</code>: "Card identity without effects." A pure embedding lookup forces the network to re-learn card mechanics from scratch every time vocab grows. Adding <code>[cost, is_attack, is_skill, is_power, x_cost, ...]</code> alongside the embedding gives the network a side-channel of fixed mechanics that doesn't need to be re-learned per card. Strike+ deals 9 instead of 6, and the embedding has to learn that — but the fact that Strike is a 1-cost attack is hardcoded in the extras and applies to every attack the bot ever sees.</p>

  <p>The keyword flags are the slot that gets the most "wait, why that one and not this one" questions. The five chosen — exhaust, ethereal, innate, retain, sly — each change optimal play in a way the embedding can't easily recover from generic experience:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>exhaust</strong> changes deck composition for the rest of the combat.</li>
    <li><strong>ethereal</strong> is a "you must play this turn or lose it" forcing function.</li>
    <li><strong>innate</strong> changes the opening-hand distribution.</li>
    <li><strong>retain</strong> changes turn-end discard.</li>
    <li><strong>sly</strong> is the trigger for Silent's discard-payoff cards (Acrobatics, Calculated Gamble) — the "play me, then discard me, then replay me" loop. Worth noting: the dataclass comment in <code>EncodedState</code> calls this out explicitly because Sly is <em>not</em> the end-of-turn-discard keyword. That is Ethereal. The naming overlap is a Slay-the-Spire trap I personally walked into and added the comment to keep myself from re-walking into it next time.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Notably absent: damage. The card embedding doesn't get a <code>damage</code> scalar feature. This is deliberate — damage is upgrade- and condition-dependent (an attack's effective damage scales with Strength, gets halved by Weak, doubled by Vulnerable on the target, etc.) and the network has all the information needed to compute it from the embedding plus the player/enemy state. Putting "raw card damage" in the input would be either redundant (vs. the embedding) or wrong (vs. effective damage). Drop it.</p>

  <p>The C# side does send a <code>target_type</code> field for each card in <code>StateSerializer.cs</code> (e.g. <code>AnyEnemy</code>, <code>Self</code>, <code>AllEnemies</code>) but the Python side doesn't currently consume it. Targeting constraints are enforced by the action mask (only legal <code>(card, target)</code> pairs are unmasked), so the network learns what targets each card permits implicitly through the legal-action mask. That isn't the cleanest factorization — making targeting an explicit input feature would let the network reason about it before the mask reaches the policy head — but it's correct, and the mask carries the same information through a different channel.</p>

  <p>The hand layout flattens to <code>B × MAX_HAND × (CARD_EMB_DIM + 11) = B × 10 × 27 = B × 270</code>. That single block is 46% of the trunk input. The hand carries the most decisional weight per turn — what to play, in what order, against whom — and the input width reflects that.</p>

  <h2 id="enemies"><span class="num">§ 4</span>Enemies — 6 slots, 17 scalars, 9-dim power pool, no positional information</h2>

  <p>Enemies follow the same pattern as the hand — pad to <code>MAX_TARGETS=6</code>, embed the ID into 16 dimensions, attach a fixed-size scalar block per enemy. The scalar block is bigger here: 17 features.</p>

  <div class="codeblock">
    <div class="code-file">src/bot/model.py · encode_state · enemies</div>
<pre><code>{`e_vals[0][i]  = e["hp"] / mhp
