  <p>Three observations.</p>

  <p>First, <code>enemy_max_hp_norm</code> exists alongside <code>enemy_hp_frac</code>. This is a small but load-bearing redundancy. <code>hp_frac</code> is <code>hp / max_hp</code> — it tells the network "is this enemy near death." <code>max_hp_norm</code> is <code>min(max_hp, 1000) / 1000</code> — it tells the network "is this enemy a chump or a boss." Without the second feature, a 5-HP-out-of-10 cultist and a 100-HP-out-of-200 elite look identical to the network. With it, they don't. The <code>min(., 1000)</code> clamp is a saturation choice, the same shape as the turn cap: nothing past 1000 max HP is meaningfully distinguishable from anything else past 1000 max HP, so cap it and stop spending input bandwidth.</p>

  <p>Second, the four <code>intends_*</code> flags are the fix for blindspot #1, the one with the longest history. The first version of <code>encode_state</code> sent only <code>intent_damage</code> — a single float per enemy. The network was excellent at "the enemy will hit me for X." It was completely blind to "the enemy will do nothing visible-as-damage but will Weak me, halving my next turn's attacks." The fix wasn't subtle: add <code>intends_attack</code>, <code>intends_buff</code>, <code>intends_debuff</code>, <code>intends_block</code> as four binary flags. The C# side already had the IntentType enum; it was just being thrown away on the Python side. (<code>StateSerializer.cs</code> actually now sends nine more <code>intends_*</code> flags — Summon, Sleep, Heal, CardDebuff, StatusCard, DeathBlow, Stun, Escape, Hidden — but the encoder currently consumes the four canonical ones. The richer set is shipped because adding them on the C# side is free and they may become consumed in a later curriculum stage; right now they pass through the wire and are dropped at the encoder.)</p>

  <p>Third — and this is the one that's easy to miss — enemies don't have positional information. The slot a given enemy occupies in the <code>enemies[]</code> array is whatever order <code>combatState.HittableEnemies</code> returns them in. The network doesn't know "the Cultist on the left" vs. "the Cultist on the right" — it only knows "two Cultists, in some order." This is mostly fine because Slay-style combat is positionally invariant (positioning matters for a few specific enemies and a small set of cards, but most of the time the targeting is by-index-within-the-fight, not by-physical-slot). The action mask pairs each <code>(card_idx, target_idx)</code> pair with the enemy at that target index in this turn's state, so as long as the order is stable within a turn, the network gets a consistent signal. It's not stable across turns when enemies die — the survivors shift left — but the network sees a fresh state per turn anyway, so the consistency window is exactly one decision.</p>

  <p>The 9-dim per-enemy generic-power pool sits at the end of each enemy's slot. Eight dimensions are the mean-pooled embedding of all non-dedicated powers on that enemy (Curl Up, Anger, Mode Shift, Ritual when not separately surfaced, etc.). The ninth is the mean-pooled normalized amount across those powers. This is the fix for blindspot #3 — enemy powers were originally invisible. The fix isn't to enumerate every enemy power into its own scalar (the long tail is enormous; there's a power for every quirky monster in the game) — it's to give the network a learned embedding table for power IDs and let it pool the active ones into a fixed-size summary. Same trick the piles use, scaled down.</p>

  <p>The whole enemy block flattens to <code>B × MAX_TARGETS × (16 + 17 + 9) = B × 6 × 42 = B × 252</code>. Padding slots are zeroed via <code>enemy_mask</code> so they contribute nothing to the trunk. A single-enemy fight still pays the full 252 dimensions; the trunk handles the masking with a multiply, not a shape change.</p>

  <h2 id="piles"><span class="num">§ 5</span>The piles — bag-of-IDs, mean-pooled, no order</h2>

  <p>The draw, discard, and exhaust piles are the place where the encoding choice differs most from the hand. Piles are encoded as bags. Each pile gets its own padded ID array (<code>MAX_DRAW=30</code>, <code>MAX_DISCARD=30</code>, <code>MAX_EXHAUST=30</code>), each ID is looked up in a separate <code>pile_emb</code> embedding table (8-dim, smaller than the hand's <code>card_emb</code> because the network needs less per-card resolution for cards it isn't currently making decisions about), and then the whole pile is mean-pooled into a single 8-dim vector before it ever reaches the trunk:</p>

  <div class="codeblock">
    <div class="code-file">src/bot/model.py · _pool_pile</div>
<pre><code>{`def _pool_pile(ids, mask):
    emb = self.pile_emb(ids) * mask.unsqueeze(-1)
    return emb.sum(dim=1) / mask.sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).clamp(min=1)

draw_pool = _pool_pile(enc.draw_ids,    enc.draw_mask)
disc_pool = _pool_pile(enc.discard_ids, enc.discard_mask)
exh_pool  = _pool_pile(enc.exhaust_ids, enc.exhaust_mask)`}</code></pre>
  </div>

