  <p>So the network sees three 8-dim vectors — one per pile — describing the average card identity in each pile. Not the order. Not the count. The count was originally encoded as the pile-size scalars at the top of the input (<code>draw_size</code>, <code>discard_size</code>, <code>exhaust_size</code>, each <code>/ 30</code>). The composition is the embedding pool. The network can in principle reconstruct "this pile has lots of attacks" from the pooled embedding even though attacks aren't a one-hot in the pool.</p>

  <p>This is the single biggest "give up information density for shape stability" trade in the encoder. Mean-pooling collapses any distribution-of-cards signal into its first moment — the network can tell "this draw pile leans toward attacks" but it can't tell "this draw pile is half attacks half powers" from "this draw pile is all medium-attackiness cards." For real Slay-style play, the second moment matters. A 50/50 mix plays very differently from a uniform-medium deck.</p>

  <p>The reason the encoding is mean-pooled anyway is blindspot #6 — pile composition was deferred to v1 by design. The original spec said draw-pile composition is "low value until the bot plays longer combats with larger decks." That call held up under early curriculum but is starting to bite at later stages, where deck size is larger and longer-horizon planning becomes valuable. The likely next iteration is a separate per-pile bag-of-types histogram (count of attacks, skills, powers per pile) appended to the pooled embedding — adds 9 dimensions total (3 pile types × 3 cardinality counts), keeps the shape contract stable.</p>

  <p>The shape contract is the reason this iteration hasn't shipped yet. The current pile encoding is <code>8 dims × 3 piles = 24 dims</code>. Adding a 3-dim count vector per pile would push that to <code>8 + 3 = 11 per pile, 33 dims total</code>, which means the trunk input grows from 583 to 592, the first Linear layer's weight matrix has to change shape, and any checkpoint trained on the old shape stops loading. Embedding-table growth is graceful (<code>resize_embeddings()</code> preserves the learned rows and only random-inits the new ones); trunk-input dim growth is a network rebuild. Every time I revisit the pile encoding I weigh that cost against the marginal-information-per-checkpoint-rebuild and end up deferring again.</p>

  <h2 id="trailing"><span class="num">§ 6</span>Relics and player powers — same shape, two scalars riding along</h2>

  <p>Relics are mean-pooled the same way piles are, with two extra scalars per relic: the normalized counter (<code>DisplayAmount / 10</code>, capped) and an <code>active</code> flag. Slots are padded to <code>MAX_RELICS=20</code>, embedded into 8-dim with <code>relic_emb</code>, masked, and pooled to a single <code>8 + 2 = 10</code>-dim summary at the end of the trunk input.</p>

