  <p>This is the cleanest encoding choice in the file because relics fit it. A typical mid-act inventory is 3–8 relics; the pool is genuinely a bag (no order); each relic's effect is identity-driven (the embedding) plus state-driven (the counter and active flag). Mean-pooling drops information about which specific combination of relics is present — but the network can't easily do anything with relic combinatorics anyway, and the pool gets the broad strokes (build leans into discard? counter relics charged up? something is currently active?) into a fixed 10-dim summary.</p>

  <p>Player generic powers are encoded the same way — <code>MAX_POWERS=15</code> slots, embedded into 8-dim with <code>power_emb</code>, mean-pooled, plus the mean of normalized power amounts as a ninth scalar. This catches everything that isn't already a dedicated player buff scalar (so Poison stacks on the player, Envenom, Footwork's stale-stacks counter, etc.). The dedicated scalar set was tuned over time — every time a new power's optimal-play implication turned out to be high-leverage, it got promoted out of the generic pool and into a dedicated slot. The C# <code>PlayerDedicated</code> set in <code>StateSerializer.cs</code> is the source of truth for which powers skip the generic pool.</p>

  <h2 id="mask"><span class="num">§ 7</span>The action mask — 61 bits and a flat indexing scheme</h2>

  <p>The action space is flat: a single 61-element boolean vector. Index 0 is <code>end_turn</code>. Indices 1 through 60 are <code>play(card_idx, target_idx)</code> pairs, encoded as <code>1 + card_idx * MAX_TARGETS + target_idx</code>. So <code>play(card_idx=0, target_idx=0)</code> is index 1, <code>play(card_idx=0, target_idx=5)</code> is index 6, <code>play(card_idx=1, target_idx=0)</code> is index 7, and so on through <code>play(card_idx=9, target_idx=5)</code> at index 60.</p>

