  <p>Every illegal action — a card that costs more energy than I have, a play targeting a dead enemy slot, a play of a non-targeted card with the wrong target index — gets <code>False</code> in the mask. The forward pass masks logits to <code>-1e9</code> before the softmax:</p>

  <div class="codeblock">
    <div class="code-file">src/bot/model.py · forward</div>
<pre><code>{`logits = logits.masked_fill(~enc.action_mask, -1e9)`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p><code>action_index_to_dict()</code> is the inverse — given an index sampled from the masked policy distribution, decode to <code>{`{"type": "play", "card_idx": ..., "target_idx": ...}`}</code> for the bridge to send back to C#. The mapping is total: every legal action in <code>legal_actions</code> (built C#-side in <code>StateSerializer.GetLegalActions</code>) corresponds to exactly one mask index, and every mask index corresponds to at most one game-side action.</p>

  <p>The thing the action mask hides is target-type semantics. A card with <code>TargetType=AnyEnemy</code> produces six mask entries (one per target slot), gated by the mask on which enemy slots are real. A card with <code>TargetType=Self</code> produces one mask entry at <code>target_idx=-1</code>, which the encoder maps to <code>target_idx=0</code> per the encoder's hardcoded convention. The C# <code>GetLegalActions</code> does the disambiguation: AnyEnemy cards produce one action per legal target; non-targeted cards produce a single action with <code>target_idx=-1</code>. The Python side trusts the legal-actions list and lets the mask carry the constraint into the network without having to re-learn targeting rules.</p>

  <p>I considered factoring the policy head into separate <code>(card_select, target_select)</code> heads — autoregressive over the action structure — but the flat 61-way head was cheaper, masked correctly, and the multi-head version would have introduced sequence-modeling complexity for almost no gain in a setting with <code>MAX_HAND=10</code> and <code>MAX_TARGETS=6</code>. Flat heads scale fine when the cardinality is in the dozens, not the thousands.</p>

  <h2 id="vs-human"><span class="num">§ 8</span>What the policy actually sees vs. what a human sees</h2>

  <p>Three asymmetries are worth flagging because they shape what the network can and can't learn.</p>

  <p>The network sees the <em>composition</em> of every pile, but not the <em>order</em>. A human player can plan around "I know my Backflip is two cards from the top because I just shuffled in a Reflex that drew it last turn." The network can't. This is partially intentional (real Slay piles are randomized; the order of cards in a draw pile is hidden information from the player too) and partially a mean-pool simplification — even the piles I do reveal order on (the discard pile, which a human can read) are reduced to a bag.</p>

  <div class="docent-row">
    <div class="docent-card">
      <div class="lb">human sees</div>
      <p>A planned-out draw — "I know my Backflip is two cards from the top because I just shuffled in a Reflex that drew it last turn."</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docent-card net">
      <div class="lb">network sees</div>
      <p>A mean-pooled bag — "draw leans toward attacks." Order is gone everywhere it's gone.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The network does <em>not</em> see card targeting types as input features — only as a constraint on the action mask. A human player sees "Strike: deal 6 damage to ANY enemy" and reasons about target choice up front. The network reasons about target choice through the combination of per-enemy state (encoded) and the masked logits (constrained). It's a different shape of reasoning, and it's measurably less efficient in early training — the network has to learn target preferences from reward, not from reading the card text.</p>

  <div class="docent-row">
    <div class="docent-card">
      <div class="lb">human sees</div>
      <p>"Strike: deal 6 damage to ANY enemy" — and reasons about target choice up front from the card text.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docent-card net">
      <div class="lb">network sees</div>
      <p>Per-enemy state plus the legal-action mask. Target preferences learned through reward, not through reading the card.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The network sees five player buffs, two combat counters, three turn-action counters, and a mean-pooled bag of everything else. A human sees the full power list rendered on the character. For powers that ended up in the dedicated set, the network has parity. For everything in the generic pool, the network sees a learned summary that probably loses a lot of fine-grained signal — and this is the place where new blindspots are most likely to surface as the curriculum expands.</p>

  <div class="docent-row">
    <div class="docent-card">
      <div class="lb">human sees</div>
      <p>The full power list rendered on the character. Every buff, debuff, counter visible.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docent-card net">
      <div class="lb">network sees</div>
      <p>Seven dedicated scalars + an 8-dim mean-pooled bag of everything else. Where new blindspots are most likely to surface.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="bought"><span class="num">§ 9</span>What this bought, and what it cost</h2>

  <p>Three things this encoder gets right:</p>

  <p><strong>Embedding tables for identity, scalar features for mechanics.</strong> Cards, enemies, relics, and powers all use the same trick: a learned embedding for "what is this thing" plus a hardcoded scalar block for "what does this thing do mechanically." The embeddings learn from reward; the scalars are correct from day zero. New cards entering vocab get a random embedding and start contributing useful signal immediately because the scalars (cost, type, keywords) are already correct — the embedding only has to learn the identity-specific bits on top.</p>

  <p><strong>Dedicated scalars for high-leverage state.</strong> The five player buffs, the four enemy intent flags, the dedicated enemy powers — none of these go through the generic mean-pooled pool. Each one has a fixed slot, a precise normalization range, and zero capacity contention with neighboring features. The generic pool exists for the long tail; it isn't doing high-leverage work.</p>

  <p><strong>Saturation where saturation is correct.</strong> Turn at 20. Max HP at 1000. Strength at ±300. Intent damage at 50. Every cap is set past the support of the data so the saturation never bites in practice, and inside the support every value lives in an interval the network can resolve.</p>

  <p>Three things it costs:</p>

  <p><strong>Pile composition is a single moment, not a distribution.</strong> Mean-pooling the piles loses the second-and-higher moments. The network can tell "draw pile leans toward attacks" but not "draw pile is bimodal." The deferred fix (per-pile type-count histograms) is on the queue.</p>

  <p><strong>Order is gone everywhere it's gone.</strong> Piles, relics, enemy powers — all bags. The network has no way to express "in this specific order." For Slay-style play this is mostly correct; for any future curriculum that adds order-dependent mechanics (positional enemies, sequencing-dependent card synergies) it's a hard limit.</p>

  <p><strong>Target-type as a constraint, not a feature.</strong> The network learns target preferences through reward and mask, not through reading the card. This works but is sample-inefficient in the early curriculum.</p>

  <p>The encoder isn't a static thing. Every entry in <code>docs/blindspot.md</code> is a place where the input shape was once wrong, the network failed to learn something a human-reading-the-card would have learned for free, and a new feature got grafted on. The current shape is the version after seven such rounds of grafting, plus a <code>SchemaVersion = 7</code> constant in <code>StateSerializer.cs</code> that gets incremented every time the JSON shape changes. The right way to read <code>model.py</code>'s constants block isn't as a design — it's as a snapshot. Each number is a bet that paid off enough not to be overwritten yet.</p>

  <div class="principle">
    <p class="quote">If I had to defend one decision in this whole encoder, it would be <code>_norm</code> with the asymmetric anchor.</p>
    <p>Six characters of code. One division. Zero clamp on the negative side. The difference between a network that knows what <code>strength=0</code> means and one that has to learn it from the data.</p>
  </div>

